1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,578
The LNG Terminal:

2
00:00:01,658 --> 00:00:05,950
The Insurance Policy
Singapore Built in 2013

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:10,602
When Indonesia trimmed the
pipeline gas it sends to Singapore

4
00:00:10,682 --> 00:00:14,868
in October 2026, the official
answer took one sentence:

5
00:00:14,948 --> 00:00:19,525
more liquefied natural gas
could be brought in at any time.

6
00:00:19,525 --> 00:00:23,774
That sentence rested on a
terminal on Jurong Island that

7
00:00:23,854 --> 00:00:27,716
had been built thirteen
years earlier, at a cost of

8
00:00:27,796 --> 00:00:32,125
1.7 billion Singapore dollars,
against exactly this day.

9
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:34,910
This post is the story of that terminal —

10
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:38,892
the companion piece to The
Pipelines That Feed Singapore,

11
00:00:38,972 --> 00:00:42,675
which told the story of the
pipes it insures against.

12
00:00:42,675 --> 00:00:48,829
By the middle of the 2000s, natural gas
made about 80% of Singapore's electricity,

13
00:00:48,909 --> 00:00:52,402
and every bit of that
gas arrived by pipeline —

14
00:00:52,482 --> 00:00:57,800
from Malaysia across the Johor Strait,
and from Indonesia's West Natuna

15
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:03,050
fields and South Sumatra, about 800
million cubic feet a day in all.

16
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:07,394
The arrangement worked, but it
had a shape that worried planners:

17
00:01:07,474 --> 00:01:10,400
two suppliers, three
pipes, no alternative.

18
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,521
A disruption to Indonesian supply in

19
00:01:13,601 --> 00:01:16,625
2004 had already caused blackouts.

20
00:01:16,625 --> 00:01:20,575
The fields themselves
would one day run down.

21
00:01:20,575 --> 00:01:24,191
And gas was still growing —
the pipelines' share of the

22
00:01:24,271 --> 00:01:27,900
country's power was heading
towards almost everything.

23
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,951
On the 7th of August 2006, the
Minister for Trade and Industry,

24
00:01:33,031 --> 00:01:38,000
Lim Hng Kiang, announced the
decision at the first EMA Forum.

25
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,988
"We need to diversify
our energy sources in

26
00:01:41,068 --> 00:01:44,484
order to ensure that we
are not over-reliant on a

27
00:01:44,564 --> 00:01:47,775
single source for our
energy needs," he said.

28
00:01:47,775 --> 00:01:50,578
"As such, the Government has decided

29
00:01:50,658 --> 00:01:53,300
to import Liquefied Natural Gas."

30
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:57,175
The plan was a terminal of
3 million tonnes a year,

31
00:01:57,255 --> 00:02:02,450
with the first gas flowing around
2012, built and run commercially.

32
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:05,300
An investor search would follow.

33
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:07,581
LNG itself deserves a sentence,

34
00:02:07,661 --> 00:02:11,925
because the whole story hangs
on what it makes possible.

35
00:02:11,925 --> 00:02:16,721
Natural gas cooled to about
minus 162 degrees turns liquid

36
00:02:16,801 --> 00:02:21,425
and shrinks to roughly one
six-hundredth of its volume.

37
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:24,420
In that form it can be loaded onto a ship

38
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:27,875
anywhere on earth and
unloaded anywhere else.

39
00:02:27,875 --> 00:02:32,000
A pipeline ties you to whoever
is at the other end of it.

40
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,311
LNG unties you —

41
00:02:33,391 --> 00:02:37,650
the supplier becomes
whoever has a cargo to sell.

42
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:42,750
An LNG trade has three links, and
they are partners, not rivals.

43
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:47,978
At the export end, a liquefaction plant
does the cooling and the loading —

44
00:02:48,058 --> 00:02:51,422
Norway's Melkøya plant,
in the photograph below,

45
00:02:51,502 --> 00:02:57,025
or Canada's new plant at Kitimat, which
shipped its first cargo only in 2025.

46
00:02:57,025 --> 00:03:00,381
In the middle, the tanker
carries the liquid,

47
00:03:00,461 --> 00:03:02,675
kept cold in insulated tanks.

48
00:03:02,675 --> 00:03:06,239
At the import end, a
receiving terminal stores the

49
00:03:06,319 --> 00:03:10,400
liquid and warms it back into
gas for the local network.

50
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:13,925
Singapore's terminal is the third kind.

51
00:03:13,925 --> 00:03:18,380
Its floating counterpart, a storage
and regasification ship moored

52
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:22,778
at a berth, does the same import
job afloat — a distinction that

53
00:03:22,858 --> 00:03:27,600
matters later in this story, when
Singapore ordered one of those too.

54
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,625
The commercial version came first.

55
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:33,688
PowerGas, Singapore Power's gas arm,

56
00:03:33,768 --> 00:03:38,053
was designated to own and
run the terminal in 2007

57
00:03:38,133 --> 00:03:41,800
and appointed its developer in April 2008.

58
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:46,566
The same month, the Energy Market
Authority appointed BG Asia

59
00:03:46,646 --> 00:03:51,412
Pacific as the LNG aggregator
— the company licensed to bring

60
00:03:51,492 --> 00:03:56,178
in and sell up to 3 million
tonnes a year of regasified LNG,

61
00:03:56,258 --> 00:04:01,025
drawn from BG's worldwide
portfolio, for up to twenty years.

62
00:04:01,025 --> 00:04:04,005
Then the global financial crisis arrived,

63
00:04:04,085 --> 00:04:06,250
and the sums stopped working.

64
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:09,739
On the 30th of June 2009,
Second Minister S Iswaran

65
00:04:09,819 --> 00:04:13,168
announced that the Government
would take over the

66
00:04:13,248 --> 00:04:17,226
terminal's development and
ownership, "as it was difficult

67
00:04:17,306 --> 00:04:20,875
to proceed with the project
on a commercial basis."

68
00:04:20,875 --> 00:04:26,049
The EMA incorporated a new company,
Singapore LNG Corporation —

69
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:31,550
SLNG — to build, own and operate
it, holding the shares in trust.

70
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:35,445
Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong,
looking back at the opening

71
00:04:35,525 --> 00:04:38,409
five years later, put
the reasoning plainly:

72
00:04:38,489 --> 00:04:42,800
"The project became too risky for
any private operator to carry.

73
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:47,697
But we believed that it was in Singapore's
strategic interests to proceed,

74
00:04:47,777 --> 00:04:51,275
and we decided that the
Government would take over."

75
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:55,908
Samsung C&T won the construction
contract in February 2010,

76
00:04:55,988 --> 00:05:00,142
and ground was broken on
the 31st of March that year,

77
00:05:00,222 --> 00:05:05,575
on about thirty hectares at the
south-western end of Jurong Island.

78
00:05:05,575 --> 00:05:08,710
The design grew while it was being built:

79
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:12,395
a third storage tank,
decided in November 2010,

80
00:05:12,475 --> 00:05:17,650
lifted the planned throughput from
3.5 to 6 million tonnes a year.

81
00:05:17,650 --> 00:05:20,182
A secondary berth project reclaimed

82
00:05:20,262 --> 00:05:22,800
about ten more hectares beside it.

83
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,623
The overall project cost came to

84
00:05:25,703 --> 00:05:28,425
1.7 billion Singapore dollars.

85
00:05:28,425 --> 00:05:30,732
The terminal took two first cargoes,

86
00:05:30,812 --> 00:05:33,200
and they are worth keeping separate.

87
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:37,156
The first cargo of any kind
was a commissioning cargo:

88
00:05:37,236 --> 00:05:42,312
bought by SLNG directly from Qatargas,
loaded at Ras Laffan in Qatar,

89
00:05:42,392 --> 00:05:45,750
and delivered on the
27th of March 2013 by the

90
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:49,935
Umm Slal — a Q-Max, then the
largest type of LNG ship in

91
00:05:50,015 --> 00:05:54,125
the world — to cool the tanks
down and prove the plant.

92
00:05:54,125 --> 00:05:59,844
Commercial operations began six
weeks later, on the 7th of May 2013,

93
00:05:59,924 --> 00:06:04,700
with BG's first cargo, carried
by the Methane Kari Elin.

94
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:08,383
The terminal that opened
for business had two

95
00:06:08,463 --> 00:06:12,898
storage tanks of 180,000
cubic metres each, one jetty,

96
00:06:12,978 --> 00:06:16,825
and a throughput of 3.5
million tonnes a year.

97
00:06:16,825 --> 00:06:20,392
By the time Prime Minister
Lee officially opened the

98
00:06:20,472 --> 00:06:24,110
terminal on the 25th of
February 2014, the third tank

99
00:06:24,190 --> 00:06:28,529
was in service and throughput
stood at 6 million tonnes a year;

100
00:06:28,609 --> 00:06:31,625
the secondary jetty
followed a month later.

101
00:06:31,625 --> 00:06:34,770
His speech is where the
insurance idea lives,

102
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:37,000
though he never used the word.

103
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,712
The terminal, he said,
"greatly increases our options

104
00:06:40,792 --> 00:06:44,718
and enhances our energy
security," and building capacity

105
00:06:44,798 --> 00:06:48,375
ahead of demand "will give
us a strategic buffer."

106
00:06:48,375 --> 00:06:52,068
He looked further ahead
still: "We are preparing for

107
00:06:52,148 --> 00:06:55,768
the possibility that our
demand for natural gas may

108
00:06:55,848 --> 00:06:59,475
one day be met entirely
by liquefied natural gas."

109
00:06:59,475 --> 00:07:02,717
At the same opening he
announced a fourth tank,

110
00:07:02,797 --> 00:07:05,200
and studies for a second terminal.

111
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:08,915
He also noted the site's
ceiling — Jurong Island could

112
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:12,570
hold seven tanks and about
15 million tonnes a year,

113
00:07:12,650 --> 00:07:16,375
"but that is the limit,
because of land constraints."

114
00:07:16,375 --> 00:07:22,435
The fourth tank, added under a 700
million Singapore dollars third phase,

115
00:07:22,515 --> 00:07:26,389
holds 260,000 cubic metres
— one of the largest

116
00:07:26,469 --> 00:07:31,600
LNG storage tanks in the world
— and entered service in 2018.

117
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,777
The terminal today has four
tanks holding 800,000 cubic

118
00:07:35,857 --> 00:07:41,117
metres in all, two jetties able to
take the largest LNG ships afloat,

119
00:07:41,197 --> 00:07:44,909
and a regasification plant
rated at an average of

120
00:07:44,989 --> 00:07:49,400
9 million tonnes a year, with
a peak of up to 11 million.

121
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:54,404
SLNG's own summary of what that
means is the policy in one line:

122
00:07:54,484 --> 00:07:58,296
if required, the terminal
has sufficient capacity

123
00:07:58,376 --> 00:08:02,825
to meet all of the country's
present natural gas demand.

124
00:08:02,825 --> 00:08:06,450
Around that core job,
a side business grew.

125
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:11,499
Trucks began loading LNG at
the terminal in April 2017 —

126
00:08:11,579 --> 00:08:15,975
the five-thousandth
truckload left in June 2024.

127
00:08:15,975 --> 00:08:19,100
Small ships reload there for the region;

128
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:23,586
Singapore's first LNG bunker
vessel, the FueLNG Bellina,

129
00:08:23,666 --> 00:08:27,753
took its first load from
the terminal in March 2021,

130
00:08:27,833 --> 00:08:32,800
and more than a hundred bunker
reloads followed in 2024 alone.

131
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:35,898
By the end of September 2023 the

132
00:08:35,978 --> 00:08:39,950
terminal had received about 430 cargoes.

133
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:43,800
The balance of supply
shifted underneath it all.

134
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:47,259
In 2013, LNG made up about 11%

135
00:08:47,339 --> 00:08:50,525
of Singapore's gas imports.

136
00:08:50,525 --> 00:08:53,854
By 2025 it was 57% — the pipelines,

137
00:08:53,934 --> 00:08:58,025
for the first time, were the smaller half.

138
00:08:58,025 --> 00:09:02,179
Insurance is only interesting
when it is claimed on,

139
00:09:02,259 --> 00:09:07,225
and this policy was claimed on
once before 2026 — at a price.

140
00:09:07,225 --> 00:09:11,277
From the 6th of September to
the 26th of December 2021,

141
00:09:11,357 --> 00:09:17,287
upstream problems in West Natuna curtailed
the pipeline supply, and low pressure

142
00:09:17,367 --> 00:09:22,025
from South Sumatra added to the
shortfall in the last quarter.

143
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:26,329
The power stations made up the
difference the way the terminal

144
00:09:26,409 --> 00:09:32,199
was built to let them: as the Ministry of
Trade and Industry later told Parliament,

145
00:09:32,279 --> 00:09:35,523
they "had to purchase
more spot LNG at elevated

146
00:09:35,603 --> 00:09:39,775
global gas prices to make up
for the drop in PNG supplies."

147
00:09:39,775 --> 00:09:41,750
The lights stayed on.

148
00:09:41,750 --> 00:09:44,350
The bill arrived instead.

149
00:09:44,350 --> 00:09:49,360
World gas prices had risen
about fivefold since March 2021;

150
00:09:49,440 --> 00:09:54,709
Singapore's wholesale electricity
price averaged 491 Singapore

151
00:09:54,789 --> 00:09:58,333
dollars per megawatt-hour in October 2021,

152
00:09:58,413 --> 00:10:04,625
against 78 Singapore dollars to 104
Singapore dollars earlier that year.

153
00:10:04,625 --> 00:10:08,979
Six electricity retailers
left the market that quarter,

154
00:10:09,059 --> 00:10:13,413
and the regulated household
tariff climbed to a peak of

155
00:10:13,493 --> 00:10:18,250
30.17 cents per kilowatt-hour
in the third quarter of 2022.

156
00:10:18,250 --> 00:10:22,698
The episode taught the precise
lesson the terminal embodies:

157
00:10:22,778 --> 00:10:26,325
LNG insures the route of
supply, not its price.

158
00:10:26,325 --> 00:10:30,540
A cargo can always be bought —
the terminal guarantees a door to

159
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:35,250
bring it through — but in a panic,
the world charges what it charges.

160
00:10:35,250 --> 00:10:38,700
The system adjusted around the lesson.

161
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:42,156
A Standby LNG Facility, a reserve stock

162
00:10:42,236 --> 00:10:45,965
arrangement managed by SLNG, was set up in

163
00:10:46,045 --> 00:10:50,125
October 2021 and made
permanent a year later.

164
00:10:50,125 --> 00:10:54,703
And in October 2023 the government
announced the deeper change:

165
00:10:54,783 --> 00:10:56,996
gas buying for the power sector

166
00:10:57,076 --> 00:11:00,625
would be centralised in
a single national buyer.

167
00:11:00,625 --> 00:11:03,956
Singapore GasCo was
established in April 2025,

168
00:11:04,036 --> 00:11:09,592
fully government-owned, under chief
executive Alan Heng — the single company

169
00:11:09,672 --> 00:11:12,632
that now buys gas for the power stations,

170
00:11:12,712 --> 00:11:18,200
pooling their demand to sign longer
contracts with more diverse suppliers.

171
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:23,277
It is the commercial half of the insurance:
the terminal is the physical door,

172
00:11:23,357 --> 00:11:27,125
GasCo is the standing arrangement
for walking through it.

173
00:11:27,125 --> 00:11:30,050
The second door is on its way.

174
00:11:30,050 --> 00:11:34,147
Announced in October 2023,
Singapore's second LNG

175
00:11:34,227 --> 00:11:38,575
terminal will not be a
bigger version of the first.

176
00:11:38,575 --> 00:11:43,090
It is a floating terminal — an
FSRU, a ship that stores LNG and

177
00:11:43,170 --> 00:11:47,831
regasifies it aboard — owned and
operated by Mitsui O.S.K. Lines,

178
00:11:47,911 --> 00:11:52,353
built by Hanwha Ocean in South
Korea with 204,000 cubic metres

179
00:11:52,433 --> 00:11:56,073
of storage and 5 million
tonnes a year of capacity,

180
00:11:56,153 --> 00:12:01,550
and moored not at Jurong Island but
at Jurong Port, on the mainland shore.

181
00:12:01,550 --> 00:12:04,688
Its steel was cut in October 2025,

182
00:12:04,768 --> 00:12:09,516
its keel laid in May 2026,
and the onshore works to

183
00:12:09,596 --> 00:12:13,950
connect it began at
Jurong Port in March 2026.

184
00:12:13,950 --> 00:12:17,320
It is due in service by
the end of the decade,

185
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:22,500
taking the two terminals together
to up to 15 million tonnes a year.

186
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:26,366
The first terminal is still
growing in its own way:

187
00:12:26,446 --> 00:12:30,313
in October 2026 SLNG broke
ground on a third jetty,

188
00:12:30,393 --> 00:12:33,875
for the smaller ships
of the bunkering trade.

189
00:12:33,875 --> 00:12:37,275
Which brings the story
back to where it began.

190
00:12:37,275 --> 00:12:41,034
When Indonesia's regulator
cut pipeline deliveries on the

191
00:12:41,114 --> 00:12:44,872
24th of October 2026 — about
31 million cubic feet a day,

192
00:12:44,952 --> 00:12:48,711
redirected to power stations
on Batam, Bintan and Sumatra

193
00:12:48,791 --> 00:12:52,685
after a long blackout — the
EMA's response the same evening

194
00:12:52,765 --> 00:12:56,591
was that Singapore GasCo "is
operationally ready to import

195
00:12:56,671 --> 00:13:00,564
more at any time," and Prime
Minister Lawrence Wong pointed

196
00:13:00,644 --> 00:13:04,550
to the LNG terminals as the
reason the country could cope.

197
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:10,082
No emergency purchase was ever
announced, and none may have been needed:

198
00:13:10,162 --> 00:13:13,825
the cut came to under 2%
of a day's gas demand.

199
00:13:13,825 --> 00:13:18,725
That is what a mature insurance
policy looks like from outside.

200
00:13:18,725 --> 00:13:22,194
Nothing happens, expensively,
for years — and then,

201
00:13:22,274 --> 00:13:26,450
on the one day it is needed,
nothing happening is the point.

202
00:13:26,450 --> 00:13:29,950
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Singapore has never

203
00:13:30,030 --> 00:13:33,132
had fuel of its own — not
the coal, not the oil,

204
00:13:33,212 --> 00:13:36,977
not the gas — so its energy
history is a history of doors:

205
00:13:37,057 --> 00:13:41,300
how many ways in, from how many
directions, under whose control.

206
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:45,975
The pipelines made three doors,
all opening onto two neighbours.

207
00:13:45,975 --> 00:13:50,129
The terminal on Jurong Island
was the first door that opened

208
00:13:50,209 --> 00:13:54,011
onto the whole world, and it
cost 1.7 billion Singapore

209
00:13:54,091 --> 00:13:58,325
dollars precisely because it
might never be strictly needed.

210
00:13:58,325 --> 00:14:02,075
The pipelines post tells
how the gas arrives.

211
00:14:02,075 --> 00:14:06,625
This one is about the door that
was built in case it stopped.
